{"id":"h253","run":2,"role":"test_wrong","category":"science-chemistry","question":"A particle of mass $1.0 \\mathrm{~g}$ is released near the surface of the Earth, where the acceleration of free fall is $g=8.91 \\mathrm{~m} \\mathrm{~s}^{-2}$. What will be its kinetic energy after  $1.0 \\mathrm{~s}$. Ignore air resistance?\nIs the correct answer \"48 $\\mathrm{~mJ}$\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"no","correct":false,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct answer to the question is No; 48 mJ is not correct.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.0867},{"text":"The particle is released from rest, so its speed after 1.0 s is v = gt.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":2.8781},{"text":"48 mJ would require v of about 9.8 m/s, corresponding to the standard g = 9.81 m/s².","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-2.0907},{"text":"The 48 mJ answer was likely computed using the wrong value of g.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.0749},{"text":"The problem explicitly states g = 8.91 m/s², which must be used.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.4923,"phi":2.8727},{"text":"Ignoring air resistance means free fall applies.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":2.875},{"text":"With g = 8.91 m/s², v = 8.91 m/s after 1.0 s.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.7,"theta":1.4923,"phi":2.8555},{"text":"The kinetic energy is ½mv² ≈ 39.7 mJ, about 40 mJ.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":2.9143},{"text":"Nothing in the physics supports 48 mJ given the stated data.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1109}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"